<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Two packages, one decision</div>
		<h1>What is in each package</h1>
		<p class="lede"><b>base</b> is the phone: the hardware, the Plasma Mobile fixes
		and the six applications written for it. <b>extras</b> is third-party
		applications you could pick for yourself.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th class="mono">Package</th><th>What you get</th></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono"><a href="#base">utsugi-surya-base</a></td><td>Kernel, audio,
			sensors, GPS, cameras, power-off, charge mode, black box, the Plasma Mobile
			fixes, and poconav, screenglaze, keepalive, app-usage, smart-unlock and
			lost-phone</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono"><a href="#extras">utsugi-surya-extras</a></td><td>Browser,
			mail, two-factor, social, photos &mdash; eight third-party packages</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono"><a href="#vayu">utsugi-vayu-base</a></td><td>The
			experimental POCO X3 Pro set. The modem does not work</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>There used to be three, and the middle one was a mistake.</b>
			A <code>utsugi-surya-full</code> sat between base and extras, and it failed in
			the only place that mattered: "full" read as more of the same as "extras", so
			the level nobody should have to think about looked optional. <code>base</code>
			carries all of it now and still answers to the old name, so a phone that has
			<code>utsugi-surya-full</code> migrates on the next <code>apk upgrade</code>
			with nothing left behind.
		</div></div>
	</div>
</section>

<section id="base">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>base &mdash; the hardware</h2></div>
		<p>Everything here exists because something did not work. It comes in three
		layers: what drives the hardware, what fixes the session, and the applications
		Plasma Mobile does not ship.</p>
		<h3>The hardware, written here</h3>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th class="mono">Package</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-boot</td><td>The kernel command line as a
			drop-in: <code>cma=512M</code> for the cameras and <code>reboot=warm</code>.
			The firmware does not keep RAM across a reboot here, so the black box writes
			to a partition instead.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-audio</td><td>Both speakers on at boot; out
			of the box only the bottom one played. The volume you left it at instead of
			booting mute, the UCM up before PulseAudio, and the <code>q6voiced</code>
			configuration for voice in call.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-sensors</td><td>Accelerometer with rotation,
			light, compass and proximity, all four through the ADSP. It seeds the writable
			copy of the sensor registry the DSP writes into, and ties the fastrpc servers to
			the device, so they start when the ADSP appears and again after it
			crashes.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-gps</td><td>Unlocks the GNSS engine that
			postmarketOS leaves locked on every Qualcomm device. Without it there is only
			network position, with kilometres of error; with it, twelve satellites and a fix
			in about forty seconds from cold.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-power</td><td>A power-off that powers off:
			it hands the shutdown to the kernel before the userspace teardown, which used
			to freeze this phone. On 2026-09-12 the teardown was measured completing on
			its own, twice, so this is a belt rather than a crutch now &mdash; and it is
			kept, because one afternoon is not enough to remove a safety net. Plus
			<code>power-reason</code>, which reads out of the PMIC why the phone turned on
			and how it turned off: <i>a person pressed the button</i>, <i>the cable</i> and
			<i>it came from a reset</i> are indistinguishable from outside, and confusing
			the first two is what kept a bug open for months. Plus the charge screen when
			you plug in a phone that is off, the hardware watchdog so a hang does not need
			a person, the USB-C direction switch (the connector was source-only and the
			phone fed the laptop), and the power-saving profile.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-camera</td><td>The rear autofocus, as a
			user-space engine against a measured curve. It has to be a service: the driver
			returns the lens to origin and powers the chip off the moment the node is
			closed, so someone must hold that descriptor open.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">utsugi-surya-blackbox</td><td>So a freeze leaves a trace:
			the console level raised above what <code>quiet</code> allows, the previous
			boot's journal archived, and <b>freeze-watchdog</b>, which
			reboots the phone when its filesystem stops answering, and writes the kernel
			log to the <code>logdump</code> partition on the way out, with
			<code>O_DIRECT</code> so it skips the filesystem that is stuck. Read it back
			with <code>blackbox --freeze</code>. The ramoops region is configured and
			comes back empty on this phone, which is why the dump goes to a partition
			instead.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Forked from upstream</h3>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th class="mono">Package</th><th>Why it is forked</th></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">linux-postmarketos-qcom-sm7150-utsugi</td><td>The kernel
			with 78 patches: the three cameras with C-PHY in CAMSS and three sensor drivers,
			voice in call through Q6VOICE, the amplifier byte order that booted the phone
			mute, 60 Hz as well as 120, the power-off, the watchdog.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">alsa-ucm-conf-qcom-sm7150-utsugi</td><td>The UCM with the
			microphone and the in-call profile this phone needs.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">hexagonrpcd-utsugi</td><td>Four patches so it serves the
			sensor registry the DSP asks to write into. Without them the sensor domain dies,
			takes the ADSP with it, and the sound card goes with the ADSP.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">iio-sensor-proxy-utsugi</td><td>Two patches that stopped the
			screen rotation from being abrupt.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">libcamera-utsugi</td><td>The software ISP enabled, the
			viewfinder sized to what it can sustain, and sensor helpers for the three
			cameras of this phone.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>Plus what the hardware needs to be driven from a shell:
		<code>tuned</code> and <code>tuned-ppd</code>, <code>qmi-utils</code>,
		<code>alsa-utils</code>, <code>i2c-tools</code>, <code>python3</code> and
		<code>firmware-xiaomi-surya</code>.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section id="session" class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>02</span><h2>base &mdash; the session and the applications</h2></div>

		<h3>utsugi-surya-plasma &mdash; the fixes</h3>
		<p>What a Plasma Mobile install on this phone is missing. None of it is a
		preference: every item was something that did not work, and it lands in
		<code>/etc/xdg</code>, the layer under <code>$HOME</code>, so anything you change
		still wins.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Piece</th><th>What was wrong</th></tr>
			<tr><td>The dialpad</td><td>Qt turned the second tap within 400 ms into a double
			click, even on another key, and the button swallowed it: dialling at a normal
			pace lost one digit in six. <code>plasma-dialer-utsugi</code> types on
			press.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Three quick settings</td><td>Location, power saving and USB power
			direction, in the pull-down where you expect them. Plasma Mobile ships none of
			the three.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>The screen turning off</td><td>There was <b>no</b> timeout configured at
			all, only the lock keys, so PowerDevil ran on factory values. All three profiles
			are written and PowerDevil is restarted, which is the only thing that makes it
			re-read them.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Notification popups</td><td>"Connection activated/deactivated" on every
			wifi-to-data switch, covering what you are reading. Silenced.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Sound on notifications</td><td>Plasma Mobile does not play the ones
			arriving over D-Bus, such as mail and messaging, and assigning a sound to its
			generic event does not work either. A watcher listens to the bus and plays
			it.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>System dialogs</td><td>The portal prompts were drawn wider than the
			screen and came out cut off on both sides, with "Cancel" outside it. That process
			gets more logical pixels, without shrinking the rest of the phone.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Bar margins</td><td>The clock and the rotation button sat under the
			rounded corners. Fixed as a device preset, which is upstreamable to KDE.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>120 Hz in the browser</td><td>Gecko does not detect this panel's
			refresh rate in a Wayland session and falls back to 60, half of what the
			screen gives: measured 59.7 fps against 115.8. The preference travels
			<b>with the browser</b>, not with this package:
			<code>utsugi-surya-plasma-firefox</code> and
			<code>-librewolf</code> are <code>install_if</code> subpackages, so apk
			adds one the moment firefox or librewolf is installed and drops it when
			they go.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>The dialer's first open</td><td>210 MB of libraries loaded on the first
			call. Preheated at session start instead.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>

		<h3>The six applications</h3>
		<p>Each one exists because Plasma Mobile does not ship it, each lives in its own
		repository, and each is built here from a tagged tarball.</p>
		<div class="grid">
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/poconav">
				<div class="n">poconav</div>
				<p><b>Car navigation.</b> Turn-by-turn with a neural-network voice
				(<code>piper</code>), routing from your own offline maps through Valhalla,
				and a screen made to be read at a glance from a dashboard rather than
				held in a hand. Plans a route, announces the manoeuvre, and re-routes when
				you miss it.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/screenglaze">
				<div class="n">screenglaze</div>
				<p><b>Screenshots, the way a phone does them.</b> Volume-down and power
				together, like Android, and then a sheet to share, save or crop what you
				took. Plasma Mobile has Spectacle, but no way to reach it without a
				keyboard.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/keepalive">
				<div class="n">keepalive</div>
				<p><b>Apps that do not die when you tidy up.</b> Mark an application and
				swiping its card away minimises it instead of closing it, so the next open
				is instant. The dialer and messages by default, costing about 125 MB
				of RAM, brings its own settings module, and opens the marked ones on
				unlock, never before it.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/app-usage">
				<div class="n">app-usage</div>
				<p><b>Battery use per application</b>, the screen Plasma Mobile does not
				have. A daemon samples what each application costs and calibrates itself
				against the battery gauge, so the numbers are measured on your phone
				rather than assumed.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/smart-unlock">
				<div class="n">smart-unlock</div>
				<p><b>No PIN when it is safe.</b> On a wifi you trust, identified by
				BSSID and gateway MAC rather than by name. Or for a while after you last
				unlocked, or inside time slots you choose. Never on by default, and it
				respects a phone locked remotely.</p></a>
			<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/lost-phone">
				<div class="n">lost-phone</div>
				<p><b>Find the phone without Google.</b> Four paths, switched on one by
				one: an SMS with your key from any phone (the only one that needs no data),
				your home network with no server at all, your own relay with a web panel
				and a map for when it is out on the street, or your ntfy. It rings at full
				volume even silenced, turns wifi and GPS on to let itself be found and
				gives them back, says where it is, and can lock or power off the phone from
				the panel.</p></a>
		</div>
		<p>Between them they pull in what they need: <code>valhalla-utsugi</code> and
		<code>maplibre-native-qt6</code> for the navigation, <code>espeak-ng</code> and
		<code>piper-tts</code> for the voice, <code>spectacle</code> and
		<code>purpose</code> for the screenshots, <code>plasma-settings</code> and
		<code>kcmutils</code> for the modules that appear in Preferences.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section id="extras">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>03</span><h2>extras &mdash; eight choices, not fixes</h2></div>
		<p>These are applications anybody could pick for themselves. They are here as one
		<code>apk add</code> for a phone that has just been flashed, and every one of them
		can be installed or removed on its own.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th class="mono">Package</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">firefox<br>mobile-config-firefox</td><td>Firefox, and
			postmarketOS's own configuration that makes it usable on a phone screen,
			with uBlock Origin. The refresh rate is <b>not</b> part of that: see
			<code>utsugi-surya-plasma-firefox</code> below.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">keysmith</td><td>Two-factor TOTP codes, native
			Kirigami.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">merkuro</td><td>Mail, calendar and contacts in one
			Kirigami application.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">tokodon</td><td>Mastodon, native Kirigami.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">neochat</td><td>Matrix, native Kirigami.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">alligator</td><td>RSS reader. Also how you follow a
			Bluesky profile, which serves RSS per account.</td></tr>
			<tr><td class="mono">koko</td><td>The photo gallery.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
		<p>Offline maps and the podcast reader were in this list until 2026-09-10, and
		came out for the same reason: Pure Maps needs <code>osmscout-server</code> holding
		the maps and <code>geoclue</code> answering "where am I", plus a download of your
		own region to be useful at all. That is a decision to make, not a set to
		preselect. <code>apk add pure-maps osmscout-server geoclue</code> and
		<code>apk add kasts</code> are one command each.</p>
		<div class="note"><i></i><div>
			<b>One thing to know before installing the set.</b>
			apk cannot leave a metapackage with an unmet dependency, so
			<code>apk del firefox</code> removes <code>utsugi-surya-extras</code> along with
			it. The other apps stay installed, but the set stops existing and no upgrade
			brings it back. Picking them one by one is the supported way.
		</div></div>
	</div>
</section>

<section id="vayu" class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>04</span><h2>vayu &mdash; experimental</h2></div>
		<p><code>utsugi-vayu-base</code> is the POCO X3 Pro set, and the honest summary is
		one line: <b>the modem does not work, so it is not a phone.</b> It carries the
		research kernel for the SM8150, the patched <code>qcom-diag</code> that measures
		the modem, the kernel command line that turns a hung task into a panic instead of
		a silent freeze, and a helper that extracts the IPA firmware from the vendor
		partition of the phone you already own, because it cannot be redistributed.</p>
		<p>It is published so the investigation is installable by anyone who wants to
		carry it further. See <a href="devices.html">Devices</a> for where it stands.</p>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="install-image.html"><b>Next</b><span>Flash the image</span></a>
			<a href="devices.html"><b>Read</b><span>What does not work</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
